# Summary
A security bug was introduced by adding release signing secrets to a job that has already executed untrusted or supply-chain-controllable code. The bug is not in GPG itself, but in the workflow trust boundary: secrets for release signing are made available after earlier steps had an opportunity to modify the runner environment used by the signing step.
The commit adds a detached GPG signing step that receives CI_RELEASE_GPG_PRIVATE_KEY and CI_RELEASE_GPG_PASSPHRASE and pipes them into gpg. This step runs in the same GitHub Actions job after dependency installation and build/test commands such as npm ci, npm run build, go test, govulncheck via go run, go install wails, and wails build. Those earlier commands execute code from the repository and third-party dependencies without access to the signing secrets at that moment, but they can still persist state for later steps on the same runner. For example, malicious dependency lifecycle code or a compromised build tool can write to GitHub Actions environment files such as GITHUB_ENV/GITHUB_PATH, set BASH_ENV for the later bash signing step, or place a fake gpg earlier in PATH. When the new signing step starts, that persisted hook can read RELEASE_GPG_PRIVATE_KEY, RELEASE_GPG_PASSPHRASE, or intercept the stdin passed to gpg, exfiltrating the release signing key. Because the generated signature and public key are then published as release assets, compromise of this key undermines the new authenticity mechanism and can allow future malicious executables to appear officially signed. The signing operation should be isolated into a separate, minimal job that does not run dependency/build code, downloads an immutable build artifact, uses tightly scoped permissions/environment protection, and preferably uses a hardware/KMS/keyless signing mechanism rather than exposing a long-lived private key to a general-purpose build runner.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit introduced a new release signing step and signature assets compared with the parent workflow.
- [x] Confirm the same job runs dependency/build-controlled commands before the signing step.
- [x] Confirm signing secrets are exposed as environment variables in that later same-job step.
- [x] Confirm the signing step invokes unqualified gpg without isolating PATH or runner state.
- [x] Demonstrate that persisted runner state from an earlier step can intercept the signing step and read the private key/passphrase while allowing the workflow to continue.
## Report
Validated the finding as a CI workflow trust-boundary/secret-exposure issue, not a memory-corruption crash. Evidence: .github/workflows/release.yml runs dependency/build-controlled commands before signing: npm ci/build at lines 34-44, go test/govulncheck/go install at lines 46-55, and wails build at lines 64-67. The commit then adds a same-job bash signing step at lines 85-107 that exposes RELEASE_GPG_PRIVATE_KEY, RELEASE_GPG_PASSPHRASE, and RELEASE_GPG_FINGERPRINT from GitHub secrets at lines 87-90 and invokes unqualified gpg at lines 98, 99, 104, and 107. Release assets include the signature and exported public key at lines 109-116, and README.md lines 29-31 instruct users to rely on these signature artifacts. The parent workflow did not contain the signing step or signature release assets, so this trust-boundary was introduced by the checked-out commit.

I built a targeted PoC that models an earlier malicious dependency/build step writing an attacker directory to GITHUB_PATH, then the runner prepending that directory before the later signing step. The fake gpg logs environment variables and stdin while emulating enough gpg behavior for the signing block to complete. Running /workspace/validation_work/ci-signing-secret-persistence-poc/poc_release_signing_path_hijack.sh produced: fake_gpg=/tmp/.../attacker-bin/gpg; signing_step_exit=0; the leak log contained RELEASE_GPG_PRIVATE_KEY=DUMMY_PRIVATE_KEY_CAN_BE_STOLEN and RELEASE_GPG_PASSPHRASE=DUMMY_PASSPHRASE_CAN_BE_STOLEN, plus stdin containing DUMMY_PRIVATE_KEY_CAN_BE_STOLEN during --import and DUMMY_PASSPHRASE_CAN_BE_STOLEN during --passphrase-fd 0. It also generated dist/ClipForVRChat.exe.asc and dist/ClipForVRChat-release-signing-public-key.asc, showing the workflow could continue successfully after interception.

Crash/Valgrind/Debugger note: a direct crash is not expected for this YAML/shell CI secret-exposure bug; the direct behavioral PoC exited 0 while leaking secrets. valgrind and gdb were checked and are not installed in the container, and they are not meaningful validation tools for this workflow trust-boundary issue.

# Evidence
github/workflows/release.yml (L109 to 116)
  Note: The workflow publishes the generated signature and exported public key as release assets, so compromise of the signing key undermines the release authenticity mechanism added by this commit.

github/workflows/release.yml (L34 to 67)
  Note: These earlier release-job steps execute npm, Go, Wails, and build commands before the signing secrets are introduced. Code run here can persist PATH or shell/environment hooks for later steps in the same job.

github/workflows/release.yml (L85 to 107)
  Note: The newly added signing step exposes the long-lived GPG private key and passphrase as environment variables and pipes them into gpg in the same job after prior code execution, allowing persisted hooks or a fake gpg to steal them.

